# MODULE.bazel.lock is ignored for now as per this recommendation:
# https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/20369
MODULE.bazel.lock

# Rust / Cargo build artifacts
#
# Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT ignored.  This tree ships a system library, so
# the lock file is committed to keep every build of it reproducible.  Do not add
# an ignore rule for it here.
#
# Cargo writes every product of the root workspace -- the three crates under
# crates/, the benchmark suites they host and all test binaries -- into the one
# target/ directory at the top of the tree, so a single anchored rule covers the
# whole library build.
/target/
# fuzz/ is excluded from the root workspace and is its own workspace root, so
# cargo gives it a target/ of its own that the anchored rule above cannot reach.
fuzz/target/
# libFuzzer scratch: crash and timeout reproducers, plus the corpus the fuzzer
# grows for itself.  Both rules stay anchored to fuzz/ on purpose -- a bare
# corpus/ would also swallow crates/zlib-rs-differential/corpus/, which is a
# committed, deterministic test fixture and has to stay tracked.
fuzz/artifacts/
fuzz/corpus/
# rustfmt backups.
**/*.rs.bk
# Scratch that the ABI verification steps write into the repository root: the
# cbindgen-generated header that gets diffed against zlib.h, and the two symbol
# lists that the nm parity diff compares.  generated_zlib.h especially must
# never be committed -- a second header sitting beside the immutable zlib.h
# would be indistinguishable from the real public contract.
/generated_zlib.h
/rust.syms
/c.syms
